#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# integer_length

#% text
# `integer_length(n)` gives the number of decimal digits of `abs(n)`.

#% text
# `integer_length(n, b)` gives the number of digits in base `b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `integer_length(0)` is 0.

#% item
# `integer_length` is listable in its first argument.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
integer_length(2026)
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
integer_length(255, 2)
#% output
#= 8

#% cell
integer_length(0)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
integer_length(-1000)
#% output
#= 4

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Large integers:

#% cell
integer_length(factorial(1000))
#% output
#= 2568

#% cell
integer_length(2^1000, 2)
#% output
#= 1001

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The length is the number of digits:

#% cell
length(integer_digits(3^50)) - integer_length(3^50)
#% output
#= 0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `integer_digits`, `log`
